# GitHub Actions + Amazon ECR CI/CD Pipeline

## About

This project demonstrates a complete CI/CD workflow using GitHub Actions, Docker, and Amazon ECR.

The goal of this project was to automate the Docker image build and delivery process instead of manually building and pushing images.

Whenever code is pushed to the GitHub repository:

- GitHub Actions automatically starts
- The Docker image is built
- GitHub authenticates securely with AWS using OIDC
- The image is pushed automatically to Amazon ECR

This project builds on previous Docker and Terraform projects by introducing CI/CD automation.

---

# Technologies Used

- AWS ECR
- GitHub Actions
- Docker
- IAM Roles
- OIDC Authentication
- Linux / WSL Ubuntu
- GitHub
- Nginx

---

# Project Structure

```bash
.
├── .github
│   └── workflows
│       └── build-and-push.yml
│
├── github-actions-ecr-cicd
│   ├── Dockerfile
│   └── app
│       └── index.html
```

---

# How the Pipeline Works

```text
Code Push
   ↓
GitHub Actions Triggered
   ↓
Docker Image Built
   ↓
Authenticate to AWS using OIDC
   ↓
Push Image to Amazon ECR
```

---

# Workflow Explanation

## 1. GitHub Actions Workflow

The workflow file is stored in:

```text
.github/workflows/build-and-push.yml
```

GitHub automatically detects workflows from this location and runs the pipeline on every push to the `main` branch.

---

## 2. Docker Build

GitHub Actions automatically builds the Docker image using the Dockerfile inside:

```text
github-actions-ecr-cicd/
```

The image contains a simple Nginx website.

---

## 3. Secure AWS Authentication

Instead of storing AWS access keys inside GitHub Secrets, this project uses:

- IAM Role
- OIDC Authentication

This allows GitHub Actions to securely authenticate to AWS without permanent credentials.

---

## 4. Push to Amazon ECR

After building the image:

- GitHub logs into Amazon ECR
- Tags the image
- Pushes it automatically to AWS

The image is then stored inside the ECR repository.

